# gatsby-plugin-sitemap 6 renamed its output: one sitemap.xml became
# sitemap-index.xml plus sitemap-0.xml. Keep the long-published URL alive.
# The target resolves through the root rewrite at the bottom of this file.
/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301!
/tech-blog/sitemap.xml /sitemap-index.xml 301!

/tech-blog/admin/* /admin/:splat 301!
/tech-blog/admin/ /admin/ 301!
/tech-blog/admin /admin/ 301!
/tech-blog/a /admin/ 301!

https://tech.boldare.com/tech-blog/* https://boldare.com/tech-blog/:splat 301
https://tech.boldare.com/ https://boldare.com/tech-blog/ 301

# The deployed layout is MIXED, so both directions are needed.
#
# Gatsby 5's adapter prefixes every *route* with pathPrefix
# (gatsby/dist/utils/adapter/manager.js), so pages and webpack bundles live
# under /tech-blog/. Files copied from static/ do not move: /img/*, /icons/*,
# /avatar.jpg and Gatsby's hashed /static/* stay at the root.
#
# Netlify only applies a non-forced rule when no file matches the request, and
# that shadowing is what keeps these two from fighting:
#
#   /tech-blog/foo/      page, nested       -> file wins, rule 1 never runs
#   /tech-blog/img/x.png static, at root    -> no file, rule 1 -> /img/x.png
#   /foo/                page, via proxy    -> no file, rule 2 -> /tech-blog/foo/
#   /img/x.png           static, at root    -> file wins, rule 2 never runs
#
# Rule 1 resolves the prefixed URLs the HTML emits onto root-level static files.
# It is the rule Gatsby 2 needed for everything; under Gatsby 5 only static
# assets still need it.
/tech-blog/* /:splat 200

# Rule 2 answers at the root, because www.boldare.com proxies with the prefix
# STRIPPED -- "/tech-blog/* https://tech.boldare.com/:splat 200" in nyc-spa's
# static/_redirects. A reader on www.boldare.com/tech-blog/foo/ arrives here as
# /foo/, which is not where Gatsby 5 puts the page. Must stay last: it matches
# everything.
/* /tech-blog/:splat 200
